Now running the test I get a "Precondition failed: The actual value of 'x' does not equal to the input value." 

I don't know why the PexAssumeUnderTest is failing. The value of the x coming in seems fine, it matches the value in the list at the index. Plus I have used the index and other values within the array list in my tests before and so I know they are fine.
Any hints on what might be causing this failure?

A: The problem you're experiencing has to do with the way .NET's ArrayList class implements its "Contains" functionality. When you use the "Contains" method on an ArrayList, it calls the "Equals" method on each object in the list until it finds a match, which means it uses reference equality (i.e., it checks for object identity, not value equality). 

So if you try to PEX test the "Contains" method, it won't be able to find paths that exercise it unless it can generate equivalent ArrayList instances with identical contents (i.e., identical elements with identical values). 

In your case, when PEX generates a value for 'x', it generates an ArrayList with an object with the same value as 'x' in it, but different instances (i.e., different objects in memory), so the "Contains" method fails due to reference inequality.

A workaround for this would be to convert your ArrayList to something like a List<T> where T : IComparable<T>, IEquatable<T> (where T is whatever type your ArrayList is storing). The problem with this approach would be converting your existing ArrayList to this type. 

Or you can use the Verifiers.Net open-source project which has a number of different verifiers and test generators that might be able to take care of this exact issue in a more clean and efficient way. 

Unfortunately I can't find a specific solution due to lack of your Test Method code. But the problem you faced is common with "Contains" functionality of ArrayList in .NET and you'll face similar problems with different collections in .NET.

I would recommend you to check the following:
1. Verify if PEX is capable to correctly identify the elements on the list to be contained.
2. Maybe you need to make the value being Inserted in the ArrayList a static value rather than a dynamic generated one so that PEX would handle it properly. 

Hope this clarifies your doubt.

A: The problems in ArrayList comes from PEX's interpretation of ArrayList. Even if PEX could provide a solution for this, ArrayList is generally considered a 'bad practice' of testing as it's a non-generic and non-deterministic type.
You should consider to convert your ArrayList to a List. It has the same functionality but you can use a generic list, e.g. List<int>. This will allow you to make all the assertions you want.
Also, you would need to assume your array list contains the value x, so your test setup could be something like:
public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]List<int> list, int x, int index)
{
    Assume.True(index <= list.Count && index >= 0);
    Assume.True(list.Contains(x));

And the rest of your test as you are now.
You can read more on PEX limitations on testable types in this article: https://quality.microfocus.com/articles/24746
Another solution would be to look into testing frameworks that are more aligned with a more common collection interface like I--------------------	    Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount+1 == New_arrListCount);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ inserted element  x appears at the right place in the ArrayList
	    Debug.Assert(Old_index == New_arrListIndexOf_x);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ all instances of x in the initial list appear in the new list.
	    Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ the index returned by LastIndexOf is always max(Old_arrListLastIndexOf_x, Old_index).
	    Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_index);
	    Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListLastIndexOf_x);
        }
    }
}

